  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "The mean heart rate of a group of patients during light exercise is $\\overset{\u00af}{x}=70$ beats per minute. A particular patient in this group has a heart rate of $74$ beats per minute, and his $z$-score is $z=2.5.$ Find the standard deviation of the patients' heart rates in the group.\n\nThe standard deviation of the heart rates is\n{{field-1}}\nbeats per minute.",
  "worked_solution": "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{\u2015}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{\u2015}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\overset{\u2015}{x}=70,$ $z=2.5,$ and $x=74$ into the formula and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{\u2015}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 74 & =70+2.5\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 4 & =2.5\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{4}{2.5} \\\\ \\sigma  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard deviation of the patients' heart rates in the group is $1.6$ beats per minute.",
